examples/week04/lesson01.cpp<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

class Volume {
public:
    explicit Volume(int level = 50) : level_{clamp(level)} {}

    void set(int level) { level_ = clamp(level); }
    void louder(int step = 10) { set(level_ + step); }
    int get() const { return level_; }          // const: callable on const objects
    bool muted() const { return level_ == 0; }

private:
    static int clamp(int v) { return v &lt; 0 ? 0 : (v &gt; 100 ? 100 : v); }
    int level_;                                  // invariant: 0 &lt;= level_ &lt;= 100
};

void show(const Volume&amp; v) {                     // only const members are callable here
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "volume " &lt;&lt; v.get() &lt;&lt; (v.muted() ? " (muted)" : "") &lt;&lt; '\n';
}

int main() {
    Volume v{70};
    show(v);
    v.louder(); v.louder(); v.louder();
    show(v);                                     // clamped to 100
    v.set(-5);
    show(v);                                     // clamped to 0
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>volume 70
volume 100
volume 0 (muted)
</code></pre> <p>Let's walk through the design:</p> <ul> <li><code>level_</code> is private: only member functions can touch it.</li> <li>The constructor and <code>set()</code> both go through <code>clamp</code>, so the invariant "0 ≤ level ≤ 100" holds for every   <code>Volume</code> that ever exists.</li> <li><code>get()</code> is marked <code>const</code>: it promises not to modify the object. That's what allows it to be called   on a <code>const Volume&amp;</code>. Forgetting <code>const</code> on getters is a very common mistake — the moment someone has a   <code>const</code> reference, your class becomes unusable.</li> <li><code>this</code> is a pointer to the current object. You rarely need to write it explicitly.</li> </ul>

examples/week04/lesson02.cpp<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

class Stream {
public:
    Stream(std::string name, int rate) : name_{std::move(name)}, rate_{rate} {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; "open " &lt;&lt; name_ &lt;&lt; " @" &lt;&lt; rate_ &lt;&lt; " ch=" &lt;&lt; channels_ &lt;&lt; '\n';
    }
    explicit Stream(std::string name) : Stream(std::move(name), 48'000) {}   // delegating
    Stream(bool) = delete;                                                  // forbid Stream(true)
    ~Stream() { std::cout &lt;&lt; "close " &lt;&lt; name_ &lt;&lt; '\n'; }

private:
    std::string name_;
    int rate_;
    int channels_ = 2;     // default member initializer
};

struct Point { int x = 0, y = 0; Point() = default; Point(int x_, int y_) : x{x_}, y{y_} {} };

int main() {
    Stream a{"mic", 16'000};
    Stream b{std::string{"speaker"}};   // see the "surprise" box: Stream b{"speaker"} would pick Stream(bool)!

    std::vector&lt;int&gt; paren(3, 7), brace{3, 7};
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "paren size=" &lt;&lt; paren.size() &lt;&lt; " brace size=" &lt;&lt; brace.size() &lt;&lt; '\n';

    int zero{};         // value-initialized: 0
    Point p{};          // default constructor
    Point q{1, 2};
    std::cout &lt;&lt; zero &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; p.x &lt;&lt; ',' &lt;&lt; p.y &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; q.x &lt;&lt; ',' &lt;&lt; q.y &lt;&lt; '\n';
}   // b closes first, then a
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>open mic @16000 ch=2
open speaker @48000 ch=2
paren size=3 brace size=2
0 0,0 1,2
close speaker
close mic
</code></pre> <ul> <li>Default member initializers (<code>int channels_ = 2;</code>) give a value whenever a constructor doesn't   mention that member. They remove a lot of repetition.</li> <li>Delegating constructors call another constructor of the same class.</li> <li><code>= default</code> asks for the compiler-generated version; <code>= delete</code> forbids an operation (here: no   construction from <code>bool</code>, which would otherwise convert silently to <code>int</code>).</li> <li>The destructor <code>~Stream()</code> runs when the object's lifetime ends. Order: the destructor body runs, then   members are destroyed in reverse declaration order.</li> </ul> <p>A real surprise we hit while writing this lesson</p> <p>Our first draft said <code>Stream b{"speaker"};</code> — and it failed to compile with "call to deleted constructor". Why? A string literal is a <code>const char*</code>, and <code>const char*</code> → <code>bool</code> is a standard conversion, which beats the user-defined conversion <code>const char*</code> → <code>std::string</code>. So overload resolution picked <code>Stream(bool)</code>. Without the <code>= delete</code>, it would have silently compiled and called the wrong constructor. That's precisely why deleting the <code>bool</code> overload is a good idea — and why <code>std::string_view</code> parameters are often friendlier than <code>std::string</code> ones.</p>

examples/week05/lesson01.cpp<pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &lt;cstddef&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;

class Buffer {
public:
    explicit Buffer(std::size_t n) : data_{new float[n]{}}, n_{n} { std::cout &lt;&lt; "  ctor(" &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; ")\n"; }
    ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; }

    Buffer(const Buffer&amp; other) : data_{new float[other.n_]}, n_{other.n_} {   // deep copy
        std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + n_, data_);
        std::cout &lt;&lt; "  copy ctor\n";
    }
    Buffer&amp; operator=(Buffer other) {          // copy-and-swap: `other` is already a copy
        std::cout &lt;&lt; "  copy assign (swap)\n";
        swap(*this, other);
        return *this;
    }                                          // old contents die with `other`
    friend void swap(Buffer&amp; a, Buffer&amp; b) noexcept {
        using std::swap;
        swap(a.data_, b.data_);
        swap(a.n_, b.n_);
    }

    float&amp; operator[](std::size_t i) { return data_[i]; }
    std::size_t size() const { return n_; }

private:
    float* data_;
    std::size_t n_;
};

int main() {
    Buffer a{4};
    a[0] = 1.5f;
    Buffer b = a;            // copy constructor
    b[0] = 9.0f;             // independent copy
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "a[0]=" &lt;&lt; a[0] &lt;&lt; " b[0]=" &lt;&lt; b[0] &lt;&lt; '\n';

    Buffer c{2};
    c = a;                   // copy assignment
    Buffer&amp; alias = c;
    c = alias;               // self-assignment (through an alias) is safe
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "c.size()=" &lt;&lt; c.size() &lt;&lt; " c[0]=" &lt;&lt; c[0] &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>  ctor(4)
  copy ctor
a[0]=1.5 b[0]=9
  ctor(2)
  copy ctor
  copy assign (swap)
  copy ctor
  copy assign (swap)
c.size()=4 c[0]=1.5
</code></pre> <p>The copy-and-swap idiom for assignment: take the parameter by value (that makes the copy), then swap our contents with it. The old contents get destroyed with the parameter. It's automatically safe for self-assignment (<code>a = a</code>) and gives the strong exception guarantee (Week 9): if the copy throws, <code>*this</code> is untouched. It's not always the fastest, but it's very hard to get wrong.</p> <p>Pro tip: the best Rule of Three is no rule at all</p> <p>If <code>Buffer</code> held a <code>std::vector&lt;float&gt;</code> instead of a raw pointer, you'd write none of these functions and copying would just work. That's the Rule of Zero, coming in Lesson 5.3.</p>

examples/week09/lesson02.cpp<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

class Playlist {
public:
    void add_bad(const std::string&amp; t) {
        ++count_;                                  // state changed...
        validate(t);                               // ...then a throw: count_ is now wrong
        tracks_.push_back(t);
    }
    void add_good(const std::string&amp; t) {
        validate(t);                               // all throwing work first
        tracks_.push_back(t);                      // strong guarantee by itself
        ++count_;                                  // no-throw commit
    }
    void replace_all(std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; fresh) {   // strong: build aside, swap in
        for (const auto&amp; t : fresh) validate(t);
        tracks_.swap(fresh);
        count_ = static_cast&lt;int&gt;(tracks_.size());
    }
    bool consistent() const { return count_ == static_cast&lt;int&gt;(tracks_.size()); }
private:
    static void validate(const std::string&amp; t) { if (t.empty()) throw std::invalid_argument{"empty title"}; }
    std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; tracks_;
    int count_ = 0;
};

struct Thrower { Thrower() = default; Thrower(Thrower&amp;&amp;) {} };
struct Safe { Safe() = default; Safe(Safe&amp;&amp;) noexcept {} };

int main() {
    Playlist p;
    try { p.add_bad(""); } catch (const std::exception&amp; e) { std::cout &lt;&lt; "add_bad threw: " &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; '\n'; }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "consistent after add_bad? " &lt;&lt; std::boolalpha &lt;&lt; p.consistent() &lt;&lt; '\n';

    Playlist q;
    try { q.add_good(""); } catch (const std::exception&amp;) {}
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "consistent after add_good? " &lt;&lt; q.consistent() &lt;&lt; '\n';

    q.add_good("So What");
    try { q.replace_all({"A", "", "C"}); } catch (const std::exception&amp;) {}
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "consistent after failed replace_all? " &lt;&lt; q.consistent() &lt;&lt; '\n';

    std::cout &lt;&lt; "noexcept(Thrower move)=" &lt;&lt; std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v&lt;Thrower&gt;
              &lt;&lt; " noexcept(Safe move)=" &lt;&lt; std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v&lt;Safe&gt; &lt;&lt; '\n';
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "noexcept(p.consistent())=" &lt;&lt; noexcept(p.consistent()) &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>add_bad threw: empty title
consistent after add_bad? false
consistent after add_good? true
consistent after failed replace_all? true
noexcept(Thrower move)=false noexcept(Safe move)=true
noexcept(p.consistent())=false
</code></pre> <p><code>add_bad</code> updates the count first, then does the throwing work. <code>add_good</code> does all throwing work on the side, then commits with operations that can't throw. That's the general recipe: do the work, then commit with no-throw operations. Copy-and-swap (Week 5) is the same idea for assignment.</p>

examples/week09/lesson05.cpp<pre><code>#include &lt;cassert&gt;
#include &lt;climits&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
